---
title: Gianduja Mousse (Bavarian-style)
yield: 1 square frame (20 cm x 20 cm)
---

= Preparation

Submerge @gelatin leaves{4%g} in #bowl{} of ice-cold @water{} for ~{5 to 10%minutes} before starting.

= Crème Anglaise Base

In a #small saucepan{}, bring @whole milk{125%ml} to a simmer. 

Meanwhile, whisk @egg yolks{60%g} and @caster sugar{25%g} together in a #bowl{} until combined (do not over-whisk into a foam). 

Slowly pour the hot milk over the yolks while whisking constantly. Return the mixture to the #pan{}.

Heat the mixture over medium-low heat, stirring constantly with a #spatula{}. Cook until it reaches 83°C. It should lightly coat the back of a spoon. Immediately remove from heat.

= Flavor Incorporation

Squeeze the excess water from the bloomed gelatin and whisk it into the hot custard. 

Place @milk chocolate{150%g}(finely chopped) and @praline{100%g}(smooth) in a #large bowl{}. 

Pour the hot custard over the chocolate and paste. Let it sit for ~{1%minute}, then whisk from the center outward until glossy and smooth.

Let this chocolate-hazelnut base cool down until it reaches 32°C.

= Final Assembly

In a separate #bowl{}, whisk cold @heavy cream{300%ml} to soft peaks (thick yogurt consistency). 

Gently fold the whipped cream into the chocolate base in three additions using a #spatula{} to retain the air.

Pour the mousse into a #20 cm square cake frame{} over the base layer. Use an #offset spatula{} to level the top perfectly flat.

Freeze for ~{4 to 6%hours} (or ideally overnight) before removing the frame and slicing.